WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:02.000
Testing should begin away from production.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.470
A lab or staging environment lets teams validate protocol support, library behavior,

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.995
certificate handling, client compatibility, performance, logging,

00:00:09.995 --> 00:00:12.000
and operational dashboards before users are affected.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.970
For TLS, it is important to test real client types: browsers, mobile apps, partner SDKs,

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:21.005
command-line tools, service clients, monitoring agents, proxies,

00:00:21.005 --> 00:00:24.250
and any enterprise interception or inspection components that sit in the path.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:27.215
Performance testing should measure the parts that could change.

00:00:27.215 --> 00:00:32.790
Teams should look at handshake size, handshake latency, CPU use, memory, connection rates,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:37.000
certificate chain size, retry behavior, failure rates, and effects on high-volume APIs.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.440
A small increase in handshake overhead may be acceptable for one service and costly for

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.000
another.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.245
Measure under realistic load, with realistic clients,

00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:46.180
through the same gateways and proxies used in production.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.975
Deployment should be staged.

00:00:47.975 --> 00:00:52.000
Canary releases, limited endpoint groups, internal-only pilots, regional rollouts,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.965
or opt-in client populations can expose compatibility issues before a broad change.

00:00:55.965 --> 00:00:59.000
Observability matters as much as cryptographic selection.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.700
Teams should alert on TLS negotiation failures, certificate validation errors,

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.000
handshake latency, client disconnects, elevated retries, unexpected fallback behavior,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.000
and service-level impacts that might otherwise look like generic availability problems.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Rollback planning is part of responsible change management.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.400
A team should know what setting to reverse, who approves the rollback,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.970
what logs must be preserved,

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:22.965
and how to avoid oscillating between configurations without understanding the failure.

00:01:22.965 --> 00:01:28.000
After deployment, monitoring should continue because client populations change over time.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.660
PQC readiness for TLS is operational discipline: test carefully, deploy gradually,

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.000
observe clearly, and document what was learned.
